## In brief
Innate lymphoid cells (ILCs) play important roles in the innate immune responses. Xiong et al. show that synergistic expression of Runx1 and Hoxa9 in embryonic stem cells induced the generation of hematopoietic progenitors capable of developing into ILC progenitors, which can further differentiate into mature functional ILCs in multiple tissues.
Article Identification and characterization of innate lymphoid cells generated from pluripotent stem cells
## SUMMARY
Innate lymphoid cells (ILCs) play important roles in regulating tissue homeostasis and innate immune responses. Generation of ILCs after engraftment of pluripotent stem cell (PSC)-derived hematopoietic progenitors (iHPCs) has not yet been reported. Here, we document that ILCs exist in \(Rag2^{-/-}/\bar{H}2g r^{-/-}\) recipients engrafted with PSC-derived iHPCs guided by Runx1 and Hoxa9 expression. Upon transplantation, iHPCs immediately give rise to ILC-related progenitors containing common helper ILC progenitors in the bone marrow, followed by a more restricted population named ILC progenitors, which are able to further differentiate into mature ILCs in the primary and secondary immunodeficient recipients. The PSC-derived ILCs exhibit multiple tissue distributions and normal immunological functions. Single-cell transcriptomics illustrates the developmental trajectory of PSC-derived ILCs in vivo, which is consistent with that of natural ILCs. Our study provides insights into the generation of ILCs in animals transplanted with PSC-derived iHPCs as a cell source.
## INTRODUCTION
Innate lymphoid cells (ILCs) are the most recently discovered subset of lymphocytes that distribute throughout the body and are particularly abundant at mucosal structures such as intestine, liver, skin, lung, and digestive tract (Artis and Spits, 2015; Bellanti and Settipane, 2019). ILCs have important functions in protecting epithelial barriers against infections and in maintaining organ homeostasis (Bando and Colonna, 2016; Cording et al., 2016; Mazzurana et al., 2018). The dysregulation of ILCs may lead to immune pathologies such as allergy and autoimmune diseases. Over the past 30 years, natural killer (NK) cells are the major known ILCs that exhibit cytotoxic activity against tumor cells (Kiessling et al., 1975). However, several new ILC populations have been identified in recent years. In contrast to the conventional lymphoid lineages such as T and B cells, ILCs lack antigen-specific receptors and do not undergo genomic receptor rearrangements or clonal selection. Despite the features that set them apart from adaptive lymphocytes, ILCs transcriptionally and functionally parallel T cells. Based on the expression of master transcription factors (TFs) and the production of effector cytokines, ILCs are mainly categorized into three groups (Spits et al., 2013; Vivier et al., 2018). Group 1 ILCs (ILC1s) comprise NK cells and the prototypical ILC1s. ILC1s express the TF T-bet and produce the cytokine interferon (IFN)-\(\gamma\), a potent stimulator of mononuclear phagocytes for antimicrobial activity effectively controlling intracellular infections. Group 2 ILCs (ILC2s) are characterized by the expression of TF GATA3 (Mjosberg et al., 2012) and produce interleukin-13 (IL-13), IL-5, IL-4, and IL-9 in response to IL-33 and IL-25 stimulation (Price et al., 2010). ILC2s can promote the immune response to helminths and serve an important pathogenic role in inflammatory lung diseases (Monticelli et al., 2011; Neill et al., 2010), Group 3 ILCs (ILC3s) express the TF ROR\(\gamma\)t and produce IL-22 and IL-17A in response to IL-23 (Klose et al., 2013; Sawa et al., 2010). ILC3s are absolutely required for the defense against intestinal bacterial infections (Magri et al., 2014). Recent studies have indicated high plasticity among different ILC subsets. A fraction of ILCs can modulate their cytokine secretion after the differential expression of cytokine-regulating TFs depending on the tissue microenvironment and inflammatory context. The observations of high plasticity of ILCs are observed between ILC1s and ILC3s (Bernink et al., 2015; Vonarbourg et al., 2010). High plasticity of ILC2s has also been reported and shown to differentiate to ILC1s or ILC3s (Bal et al., 2016; Ohne et al., 2016; Silver et al., 2016).
