All subsets of ILCs and adaptive lymphocytes are proposed to be derived from lymphoid-myeloid primed progenitors (LMPPs) and common lymphoid progenitors (CLPs), followed by stepwise
commitments to each lymphocyte fate (Kondo et al., 1997; Zook and Kee, 2016). ILC lineage commitment requires the expression of inhibitor of DNA binding 2 (Id2) to repress E-box proteins that will otherwise result in T or B cell commitment from CLPs. The differentiation of ILCs from CLPs has suggested a progression of more restricted progenitors defined by their cell-surface markers and TF expression (Walker et al., 2019). Downstream of CLPs are the common innate lymphoid progenitors that can give rise specifically to all ILCs containing NK cells (Yokota et al., 1999). Further downstream are the common helper ILC progenitors (CHILPs) that give rise to lymphoid tissue inducer cells and the more restricted common ILC progenitors (ILCPs). ILCPs can produce cells committed to one of the ILC subsets, including the ILC1, ILC2, and ILC3 subsets. A lineage-restricted ILC2 progenitor (ILC2Ps) have been identified in the bone marrow on the basis of expressing the signature TF GATA3, along with many other ILC2 markers such as ICOS, CD25, and IL-1RL1 (Hoyler et al., 2012). The ILC1 progenitors (ILC1Ps) and ILC3Ps are found downstream of the ILCPs and can efficiently generate lineage-restricted ILC1s and ILC3s, respectively. But some studies have shown that mixed ILC1 and ILC3 progenitors (ILC1/3Ps) expressing both ILC1- and ILC3-related genes can differentiate into mature ILC1s and ILC3s in vivo or in vitro (Walker et al., 2019).
Although many studies have been made in understanding the origins, developmental trajectory, and functions of ILCs in recent years, research on the generation of ILCs from pluripotent stem cells (PSCs) has not yet been reported. Our previous studies have shown that the induced hematopoietic progenitor cells (iHPCs), derived from embryonic stem cells with synergistic and inducible expression of Runx1 and Hoxa9 (iR9-ESCs), can generate functional T cells accompanied with some detectable NK cells (Guo et al., 2020). In this study, we explored whether we could obtain the other ILC subsets using the differentiation system mentioned above. Here, we showed that iR9-ESCs induced generation of engraftable iHPCs in vitro capable of developing into mature ILCs in the \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipients. The induced ILCs (iLCs) distributed normally in multiple tissues and exhibited normal immunological functions in responding to cytokine stimuli. Single-cell transcriptome analysis of the iILCrelated progenitors in the same marrow illustrated the trajectory of iLC development from iR9-ESCs. Upon transplantation, the iHPCs immediately gave rise to lymphoid progenitors, following differentiation into ILCPs, and subsequently developed into ILC2Ps and ILC1/3Ps, which were able to further commit to multiple mature iLCs. We establish a de novo approach for generating functional iLCs in vivo using PSC-derived iHPCs, which provides an important reference for in-depth research on ILCs in the future.
## RESULTS
## ILC generation in vivo from inducible Runx1-p2a-Hoxa9- edited embryonic stem cells
To induce ILC generation, we followed a stepwise strategy established before (Guo et al., 2020). The Runx1-p2a-Hoxa9-edited embryonic stem cells (iR9-ESCs) have been constructed by introducing the inducible expression cassette of Runx1-p2a-Hoxa9
into the Rosa26 locus of a GFP-transgenic mouse embryonic stem cell line (C57BL/6 background). Doxycycline was added to induce the expression of Runx1 and Hoxa9. Following the protocol (Figure 1A), we successfully generated hemogenic endothelial progenitors (iHECs, \(\mathsf{G F P^{+}C D31^{+}C D41^{l o w}C D45^{-}C-K i t^{+}C D201^{+}})\) from iR9-ESCs (Figures S1A and S1C). After co-culture of these iHECs with notch-ligand-expressing OP9 stromal cells (OP9-DL1) in the presence of conditioned medium derived from supernatants of AF024 (mIL3/bElt3/mSCF) cell culture and doxycycline (1 \(\mu\)g/mL), iR9-ESC-derived iHPCs (R9-iHPCs) occurred at day 21 (Figures S1B and S1D), including induced CHILPs (iCHILPs) (Figure S1E), and few iILC1s and iLC2s, but no iLC3s (Figures S1F and S1G). To functionally assess the engraftment potential of R9-iHPCs, we transplanted three million R9-iHPCs at day 21 into irradiated (2.25 Gy) ILC-deficient \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice with continuous doxycycline \((1\mathrm{~mg/mL})\) water feeding. iR9-ESC-derived \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}C D127^{+}R O R y t^{-}N K p46^{+}}\) iLC1s and \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}C D127^{+}R O R y t^{+}}\) iLC3s (Gury-BenAri et al., 2016), as well as ST2-expressing \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}C D90.2^{+}S T2^{+}}\) iLC2s (Pu et al., 2021), were detected in the intestine of \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice at 4 weeks after transplantation (Figures 1B and 1C). We further investigated the kinetics of all R9-ESC-derived mature iLC subsets in the intestine. Through the statistics of several independent experiments, it was found that the proportion of iR9-ESC-derived \((\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}})\) iLC1s gradually increased from about \(25\%\) at 2 weeks after transplantation to \({>}90\%\) at 3 weeks after transplantation and then gradually decreased from 4 weeks to 10 weeks after transplantation (Figure 1D). The proportion of iLC3s in \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}}\) cells gradually increased with the time after transplantation and reached the maximum at week 5 but almost disappeared at week 10 (Figure 1E). In addition, the proportion of iLC2s in \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}}\) cells peaked at week 5 after transplantation and decreased thereafter (Figure 1F). The induced \(\mathsf{N K1.1^{+}}\) NK (iNK) cells and CD3/CD4/ \({\mathsf{C D}}8^{+}\) T (iT) cells were also detected in the intestine, and the frequency of iT cells gradually increased from week 3 and reached \({>}90\%\) at week 10 after transplantation (Figures S2A and S2B). Furthermore, genomic PCR sequencing using primer pairs flanking the Runx1-p2a-Hoxa9 element confirmed that the iLCs in vivo were of iR9-ESCs origin, which carried the inserted Runx1-p2a-Hoxa9 element (Figures S3A–S3C). Thus, we established an approach of generating iLCs from gene-edited ESCs.
## The iR9-ESC-derived iLCs show features of multi-organ distributions
We further analyzed the tissue distributions of the iR9-ESC-derived iLCs in \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice. We performed flow cytometry analysis of the cells obtained from different lymphoid tissues, including liver, spleen, lung, and thymus. Mature iLC1s \((\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}N K1.1^{+}N K p46^{+}C D49a^{+}C D49b^{-}})\) (Fuchs et al., 2013; Wang et al., 2018), ST2-expressing iLC2s \((\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}C D90.2^{+}S T2^{+}})\), and KLRG1-expressing inflammatory iLC2s \((\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}K L R G1^{+}S T2^{-}})\) were detected in the liver (Figures 2A and 2B) and spleen (Figures 2C and 2D) of \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice at 3 weeks after transplantation. Meanwhile, the ST2-expressing iLC2s and KLRG1-expressing inflammatory iLC2s were also detected in the lung (Figure 2E). The thymus of \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient
