## The iR9-ESC-derived iLCs are functionally equivalent to wild-type ILCs
Previous studies have demonstrated that ILCs not only have the ability to promote tissue homeostasis but can also modulate immune responses at multiple levels, some of which are mediated by IFN- \(\cdot\gamma\), IL-5, IL-22, and IL-17A cytokine secretions (Gladiator et al., 2013; Klose et al., 2013; Lee et al., 2011; Molofsky et al., 2013). To confirm that the induced mature iLCs were able to express the signature cytokines associated with ILC function, ex vivo stimulations were performed. The \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}}\) \(\mathsf{C D}127^{+}\) iLCs (R9-iLCs) containing iLC1s and iLC3s from the intestine of \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice were sorted and cultured in vitro (Fuels et al., 2013; Wang et al., 2021). The \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}L i n^{-}C D127^{+}}\) ILCs (wild-type [WT]-ILCs) from the intestine of WT mice (C57BL/6, CD45.2) were sorted and cultured as control. Flow cytometry indicated the presence of IFN- \(\cdot\gamma^{+}\) cells in the R9-iLC1s (Figure 3A), as well as IL- \(17\mathsf{A}^{+}\) cells and IL- \(22^{+}\) cells in the R9-iLC3s (Figure 3B). No significant differences in IFN- \(\cdot\gamma\) production were observed between the WT-ILC1s and R9-iLC1s (Figure 3D). We next investigated the cytokine expression within the splenic KLRG1+ R9-iLC2s and KLRG1+ WTILC2s. Slightly elevated IL-5 and significantly elevated IL-13 expression was detected in R9-iLC2s compared with the WTILC2s (Figures 3C and 3E). Although both R9-iLC3s and WTILC3s had similar IL-17A and IL-22 expression by intracellular flow cytometry (Figure 3F), the WT-ILC3s produced higher amounts of effector cytokines IL-22 compared with the R9-iLC3s (Figure 3G). In addition, a small number of IFN- \(\cdot\gamma^{+}\) iLC1s were detected in the spleen and liver (Figure 3H) of recipients after intraperitoneal IL-12 and IL-15 injections. These results indicated that the iR9-ESC-derived iLCs were activated in vitro or in vivo and could secrete corresponding cytokines in response to stimuli, suggestive of a typical immune response.
## Single-cell RNA-seq identifies transcriptional states of intestinal iLCs
To further characterize the iR9-ESC-derived iLCs (R9-iLCs) at the transcriptome level, we performed single-cell RNA
sequencing (scRNA-seq) of the entire \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}C D127^{+}}\) iLC population from the intestine of \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice and the \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}L i n^{-}C D127^{+}}\) ILC population (WT-ILCs) from the intestine of WT mice (C57BL/6, CD45.2) (Figure 4A). The other cell types were ruled out based on the lack expression of Cd5 (T cells), Cd19 (B cells), Itgax (identitic cells [DCs]), and Itgam (myeloid cells). iLC subsets were identified by the expression of ILC-associated genes (Figure 4B). Our data indicated that both R9-iLCs and WT-ILCs highly expressed \(I I Z r\), consistent with the flow cytometry results. Most sorted iLCs expressed ILC-associated surface marker-encoding genes Kit and Ly6a. The TF Id2, which expressed in high amounts in all ILC lineages (Yu et al., 2016), was detected in all single sorted iLCs (Figures 4B and 4C). NKp46 (encoding by Ncr1), a marker expressed by ILC1s that was critical for ILC1 development (Wang et al., 2018), was found expressed in R9-iLCs. Furthermore, the other genes identified in the ILC1 signature, including Klrb1b, Ncr1, Sh2d2a, Ccl5, Defa6, Rbp2, Nfil3, Foxo1, Itga4, and Top2a (Gury-BenAri et al., 2016), were expressed in R9-iLCs at levels similar to WT-ILCs (Figure 4C and S5A). Partial R9-iLCs were found to highly express the conventional ILC2-featured genes, such as Rora, Tnfrsf18, Arg1, Il2ra, Gata3, Selplg, Cd7, Bcl11b, Klrb1, and Csf2 (Ghaedi et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2012), indicating their ILC2 identity (Figures 4C and S5A). Interestingly, the gene expression value of Gata3 in R9-iLCs was much higher than that in WT-ILCs (Figure S5A). In addition, a large proportion of R9-iLCs and WT-ILCs both expressed ILC3 signature genes. This signature included well-studied transcripts such as Rorc, Ccr6, Odc1, Cd74,Tcf7, Nrp1, and Batf3 (Sawa et al., 2010), as well as several newly discovered genes such as Stom, Capg, Foxs1, and Cntn1 (Robinette et al., 2015) (Figure 4B, 4C, and S5A). Analysis of ILC-featured chemokines and chemokine receptors (Figure 4D), as well as cytokines and cytokine receptors (Figure 4E) in R9-iLCs and WT-ILCs, revealed the same expression patterns. The identities of these iR9-ESC-derived intestinal iLCs were further confirmed by mapping with a dataset acquired from the ILCs in the intestine of WT mouse (a published scRNAseq dataset, GEO: GSE85152) (Gury-BenAri et al., 2016) (Figures S5B and S5C). Together, these data illustrate the transcriptome landscape of the intestinal iLCs.
scRNA-seq reveals the transcriptome features of the iR9-ESC-derived ILCPs, ILC2Ps, and heterogeneous ILC1Ps and ILC3Ps
We observed iR9-ESC-derived ILC-related progenitor cells in the bone marrow of \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice at 7.5 days after transplantation. The phenotypic \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}S c a1^{+}C^{-}K i t^{+}}\) (LSK) progenitors, \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}S c a1^{m i d}C^{-}K i t^{m i d}C D127^{+}}\) iCLPs, \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}C D127^{+}C D135^{-}\alpha4\beta7^{+}S c a1^{-}/^{m i d}C D25^{-}}\) iCHILPs, and \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}C D127^{+}C D135^{-}\alpha4\beta7^{+}S c a1^{+}}\) \(\mathsf{C D25^{+}}\) iLC2Ps were all detected (Figures S6A and S6B). To further characterized the transcriptome landscape of the
