iR9-ESC-derived progenitors, we sorted \(\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}L i n^{-}}\) cells from the bone marrow and performed scRNA-seq (Figure S6C). The scRNA-seq dataset was processed, and 9 clusters of a total of 7,391 single cells were identified and visualized using uniform manifold approximation and projection (UMAP) based on their unique gene expression signatures (Figure 5A, left panel). Subsequently, to facilitate the delineation of ILC-related populations, the clusters (clusters 0, 2, and 3) with over \(50\%\) of cells expressing \(|d2|\) (unique molecular identifiers [UMIs] \(>1\) ) and high expression of ILC-progenitor surface maker-encoding genes Il7r, Itga4, and Itgb7 were extracted for further analysis (Figure S6D). Following identification of high variable genes and principal-component analysis (PCA), we performed clustering and dimensional reduction analysis using selected principal components (PCs). The remaining ILC-progenitor clusters (clusters 0, 2, and 3) were identified as 5 distinct populations, including clusters C1–C5 (Figure 5A, right panel). The expression of differentially upregulated genes among clusters C1–C5 was shown (Figure 5B; Table S1).
Cluster C1 was a mixed population characterized as induced lymphoid progenitor (iLP) by expressing genes associated with early lymphoid progenitor cell identity, including Cd34 (Rabe et al., 2020), ler3 (Ramsey et al., 2014), Lat2 (Zhu et al., 2012), Gata2 (Ahmed et al., 2020), and Cyp11a1 (Wang et al., 2013) (Figure 5B and 5C). Among iLP populations, a small number of iCHILPs were identified based on the expression of CHILP surface maker-encoding genes Il7r, Itga4, Itgb7, Il2ra, Kit, and Ly8a. Cluster C2 was characterized as iILCPs by the expression of a spectrum of genes previously associated with early ILC-progenitor cell identity, including Tcf7 (Held et al., 2003), Tox2 (Seehus et al., 2015), Bcl2 (Constantinides et al., 2014), and Igfbp4 (Walker et al., 2019) (Figure 5B). PD-1 has been reported as a marker of PLZF+ILCPs (Seillet et al., 2016). The widespread expression of Zbtb16 (PLZF) and Pdcd1 (PD1) in cluster C2 further suggested that they were iLCPs (Figure 5C). Cluster C3 was characterized as iILC1/3Ps by predominantly expressing ILC1- and ILC3-related genes, including Xcl1 (Walker et al., 2019), Ms4a4b (Walker et al., 2019), Ncr1 (Wang et al., 2018), Gzma (Garcia-Laorden et al., 2017), Il2rb (Yu et al., 2016), Rorc (Sawa et al., 2010), and Tbx21 (Klose et al., 2013) (Figures 5B and 5C). Notably, we observed additional substructure in the gene expression patterns within cluster C3. One subcluster was characterized by the expression of genes, including the Klr transcripts, the chemokine Xcl1, and the absence of Rora and Rorc, whereas another subcluster contained cells expressing Rora and Rorc but had an absence of Klr genes (Figure 5dE). Therefore, cluster C3 was identified as a heterogeneous population iLC1/3Ps. Cluster C4 was iLC2Ps, characterized by the expression of genes associated with the ILC2 lineage, including Areg (Monticelli et al., 2016), Stab2 (Schneider et al., 2019), Arg1 (Monticelli et al., 2016), Il17rb (Halim et al., 2012), and Gata3 (Figures 5B and 5C). Cluster C5 was characterized as iILCX that did not match any of the known ILC-related progenitors. Differential chemokine receptor expression further delineated
clusters. Cells in cluster C2 were predominantly C-X-C motif chemokine receptor 5-positive (Cxcr5+), a surface marker detected on ILCPs (Harly et al., 2018). Cluster C4 cells expressed principally C-C motif chemokine receptor 9 (Ccr9), which was primarily expressed on ILC2Ps as reported previously (Hoyler et al., 2012), and Cxcr3 was largely restricted to cluster C3 (Figure 5D).
To investigate the developmental relationship of the iLP, iILCP, iLC2P, and iILC1/3P populations mentioned above and to delineate expression patterns of the genes involved in iILC development, we performed pseudotime analysis. Using this approach, we identified that the iILC-related progenitors developed along a trajectory from the early iLPs located in C1 toward iILCPs (cluster C2) and then toward a bifurcation point at which the putative iILC1Ps and iILC3Ps (cluster C3) and iILC2Ps (cluster C4) diverged (Figure 5E). Overall, the differentiation trajectory of iILC-related progenitors was consistent with that of natural progenitors. To profiles finely distinguished the change of transcriptome along the differentiation trajectory, we identified the significant pseudotime variable genes differentially expressed across the single-cell trajectory. Analysis of the genes differentially expressed along cell fate progression from iILPs to iILC1/3Ps and iILC2Ps revealed progressive downregulation of the LP-associated genes Gata2, Lat2, Cyp11a1, Apoe, Basp1, and ler3 (Figures 5F and S7A). ILCP-related genes, including Bcl2, Tox2, Nfi3, Tcf7, Ramp1, and Batf3, showed upregulation along iLP to iILCP progression (Figures 5F and S7A). The genes Nrf1, Ms4a4b, Lihb4a, Il2rb, Klrb1b, AW112010, Cxcr6, and Runx3 expressed in ILC1/3Ps were upregulated from iILPs to iILC1/3Ps (Figures 5F and S7A). ILC2P-associated genes, including Areg, Stab2, Bcl11b, Gata3, Tph1, and Rora, also showed upregulation along their cell-fate progression (Figures 5F and S7A). We next performed gene coexpression module analysis of the pseudotime variable genes. Genes with high expression in iLPs, iLCPs, iILC1/3Ps, and iILC2Ps were segregated into 2 modules, respectively (Figure S7B). Gene Ontology analysis of the module-specific iLC-progenitor related genes indicated enrichment for genes encoding proteins involved in regulating innate immune responses (Figure S7C), consistent with previous research.
The identities of these iLC-progenitor populations (C1–C5) were further confirmed by mapping with a dataset obtained from the ILC-related progenitors in the bone marrow of a WT mouse (a published scRNA-seq dataset, GEO: GSE131038) (Walker et al., 2019) (Figures S8A–S8E). These data demonstrated that the iR9-ESC-derived iLC-progenitor populations possessed a transcription profile resembling WT counterparts. Taken together, the iR9-ESC-derived iLCs demonstrated a similar developmental trajectory of natural ILCs.
