## DISCUSSION
In this study, we demonstrated that forced expression of Runx1 and Hoxa9 in mouse ESCs (mESCs) could give rise to progenitor cells preferentially differentiating into iILCs in \(R a g2^{-/-}/I2r g^{-/-}\) recipients. The iILCs distributed normally in multiple tissues and exhibited normal immunological functions in responding to cytokine stimuli. We also transplanted the R9-iHPCs at day 21 into irradiated \(R a g1^{-/-}\) recipient mice (CD45.1, lack adaptive lymphocytes but have intact ILCs) and WT mice (CD45.1) with continuous doxycycline \((1\;\;\mathsf{m g}/\mathsf{m L})\) water feeding. The iR9-ESC-derived iCLPs were detected in the bone marrow of \(R a g1^{-/-}\) recipients at 3 weeks after transplantation (Figure S10A). We also detected mature iILC1s and iILC3s in the intestine, as well as iNK cells in the bone marrow and spleen, of \(R a g1^{-/-}\) recipients at 3, 4, and 6 weeks after transplantation (Figures S10B–S10D). In addition, we found R9-iCHILPs in the bone marrow and iILC2s in the spleen of WT recipients at 4 weeks after transplantation (Figures S11A and S11B). iNK cells were also present in the spleen and bone marrow from 4 to 8 weeks after transplantation (Figures S11C and S11D). We could not detect iILCs in the intestine, liver, and lung of WT recipients for the extremely low contribution rate of iR9-ESC-derived cells. These results indicate that the R9-iHPCs can differentiate into some specific, but not all, types of mature ILCs in \(R a g1^{-/-}\) or WT recipients. We hypothesize that the iR9-ESC (C57BL/6 background)-derived partial iLCs may fail to take advantage of the niches occupied by their natural counterparts in the \(R a g1^{-/-}\) or WT recipients (C57BL/6 background). Interestingly, in addition to iILCs, the R9-iHPCs transplanted into \(R a g1^{-/-}\) or WT recipients mainly produce B cells, whereas they mainly produce T cells in \(R a g2^{-/-}/I2r g^{-/-}\) recipients. The microenvironment of the recipient mice may play important roles in the types of immune cells produced.
The results of scRNA-seq on the entire iR9-ESC-derived iILC population showed that \({\mathsf{C D}}117^{+}\) iILCs expressed ILC1-, ILC2-,
and ILC3-associated genes, confirming the existence of the three classical subsets iILC1s, iLC2s, and iILC3s (Figure 4). The iR9-ESC-derived iILC subsets resembled their WT counterparts at the transcriptome level. ILC2s are tissue-resident cells that reside in peripheral tissues, with the local microenvironment being essential for their functions and phenotypes. Mouse ILC2s commonly express KLRG1, ST2, CD90, IL-7R (CD127), IL-2Rα (CD25), and SCA1 (Huang et al., 2015). The surface marker ST2 is used to mark natural ILC3s (nUC1s), which are considered homeostatic cells. KLRG1 is associated with inflammatory ILC2s, which respond to IL-25 by producing IL-13 (Huang et al., 2015). Although \(\mathsf{L i n}^{-}\mathsf{C D}127^{+}\mathsf{K L F G}1^{+}\) iILC2s were not detected in the intestine, both \(\mathsf{L i n}^{-}\mathsf{C D}90.2^{+}\mathsf{S T}2^{+}\) iILC2s and \(\mathsf{L i n}^{-}\mathsf{C D}90.2^{+}\mathsf{C D}25^{+}\) iILC2s were detected (Figures 1C and 1F). The iR9-ESC-derived iILC2s in the intestine were mainly \({\mathsf{S T}}2^{+}\) ILC2s. Meanwhile, we detected both iR9-ESC-derived \({\mathsf{S T}}2^{+}\) iILC2s and \(\mathsf{K L R G1^{+}}\) inflammatory iILC2s in the liver, spleen, and lung (Figures 2B, 2D, and 2E). The R9-iHPCs transplanted into the recipients give rise to different types of iILCs in different tissues.
In our differentiation system, the iR9-ESC-derived iILCs showed a transient wave in intestine that could persist for about 10 weeks after transplantation. In addition to mature iILCs, we also detected iR9-ESC-derived iT cells in the intestine of \(R a g2^{-/-}/I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice (Figure S2). Previous studies indicate that both T cells and ILCs are derived from the same progenitors, CLPs, and the similarities between helper-like ILCs and T helper cell subsets are striking (Guo et al., 2014; Liang et al., 2011). In addition, there is a degree of functional redundancy between ILCs and T cells (Rankin et al., 2016; Song et al., 2015; Vely et al., 2016). The limited activation requirements of ILCs allow them to serve as first responders during the early stages of a primary immune response, as well as produce cytokines in response to subtle perturbations during homeostasis. Therefore, we speculate that in the early stage after transplantation, the R9-iHPC-derived iILCs predominantly play roles in metabolic homeostasis and tissue repair. But iT cells are more competitive in the same microenvironment. With the time after transplantation, iT cells gradually replace iILCs to play roles in immune responses, which leads to the reduction in the number of ILCs. The proportions of iR9-ESC-derived iILCs in the liver,
spleen, and lung are also changed with time. The percentages of these iLC2s in the total hematopoietic cells \((\mathsf{C D}45^{+})\) of spleen \((0.04\%{-}0.3\%)\) and liver \((0.6\%{-}1.7\%)\) at 2–10 weeks after transplantation are higher than those observed in the spleen \((0.03\%)\) and liver \((0.5\%)\) of WT mouse. However, the frequency of iLC2s in the total hematopoietic cells \((\mathsf{C D}45^{+})\) of lung ranged from \(0.2\%\) to \(0.745\%\) , lower than those observed in the lung \((0.7\%)\) of WT mouse. Over \(90\%\) of iT cells were detected in the spleen, liver, and lung at 6 weeks after transplantation (Figure S4). Given the similarities between ILCs and T cells, it is important to understand which functions of ILCs are specialized and which are redundant compared with T cells. In our system, the R9-iHPCs initially produce only iILCs and then produce both iILCs and iT cells. The induction system provides a tool for studying the relationship between ILCs and T cells.
Previous studies have revealed high plasticity among different ILC subsets that may develop under pressure from external signals (Zook and Kee, 2016). ILC1s can differentiate to ILC3s in the presence of IL-2, IL-23, and IL- \(1\upbeta\) (Bernink et al., 2015). High plasticity of ILC2s has also been reported and shown to differentiate to ILC1s or ILC3s (Bal et al., 2016; Ohne et al., 2016; Silver et al., 2016). Additionally, recent evidence suggests the existence of an intermediate ILC subgroup between ILC1s and ILC3s (Bernink et al., 2015; Klose et al., 2013; Verrier et al., 2016; Vonarbourg et al., 2010) or between ILC1s and ILC2s (Lim et al., 2016; Silver et al., 2016), which co-express genes related to different ILC subsets. In our study, the iR9-ESC-derived iILCs from intestine show no discrete boundaries in single-cell expression profiles that neatly partitioned iILC1s from iILC2s or iILC2s from iILC3s. These iR9-ESC-derived iILCs may be at a process of mutual transformation in the intestine microenvironment or are some new ILC subsets.
scRNA-seq shows typical expression patterns of surface markers, TFs, and essential regulators in iR9-ESC-derived iLC progenitor cells, which suggests normal early iLC generation in \(R a g2^{-/-}/I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipients. In the iR9-ESC-derived iLC progenitor cells, we detected a small fraction of an ILC-related population (iILCX) that did not match any of the known ILCrelated progenitors. This cell population contained partially transcriptional features of iILCP, iLLC1/3P, and iILC2P with the expression of ILCP-related genes Ckb, Igfbp4, and Igfbp7, ILC1/3P-related genes Ncr1, Klrd1, and Xcl1, and ILC2Prelated genes Areg, Ccdc184, Csf2, and Stab2 (Figure S12A). Pseudotime analysis indicated that the iILCX population was located between iILCP and ILC1/3P along the trajectory (Figures S12B and S12C). It has been reported that there are many transition states during the development of ILCs (Walker et al., 2019). The ILCPs will pass through a plastic phase before the terminal differentiation of ILC subsets. The iILCX subset is more likely an intermediate cell population in a transition state or a plastic ILCP state.
In conclusion, this study establishes an approach of reconstituting ILCs based on a two-step approach of in vitro HPC commitment from editable PSCs and in vivo lymphopoiesis. The iILCs possess transcriptomes, hierarchical differentiation, tissue distribution, and immunological functions that closely resemble those of their natural counterparts. The induction system of ILC generation provides a tool for the basic study of ILC
development and further provides insight into the regeneration of human ILCs using human PSC source.
## Limitations of the study
In this study, we identified that forced expression of Runx1 and Hoxa9 in ESCs could give rise to iHPCs preferentially differentiating into functional iILCs in the early stage after transplantation and then producing iT cells at 3 weeks after transplantation with the percentage gradually increased over time. However, we recognized that the molecular mechanism of this process was still not explored. We anticipate that studies describing how Runx1 and Hoxa9 regulates the production of ILCs and T cells will be highly informative for the understanding of the origin of ILCs and for obtaining sufficient numbers of ILCs for cellular immunotherapy.
