## EXPERIMENTAL MODEL AND SUBJECT DETAILS
## Mice
EXPERIMENTAL MODEL AND SUBJECT DETAILSMiceC57BL/6 (CD45.1) mice were purchased from the Jackson Laboratory. C57BL/6 (CD45.2) mice were purchased from the GemPharmatech Co., Ltd. \(R a g2^{-/-}/I I2r g^{-/-}\) (B-NDG, NOD-PrkdcScidIL2rgtm1/Bcgen, CD45.1) mice were purchased from Biocytogen Jiangsu Co., Ltd (Jiangsu, China). \(R a g1^{-/-}\) mice (C57BL/6) were a gift from Z. Liu from Institute of Biophysics (CAS, China) and from Model Animal Research Center of Nanjing University. We used 8- to 12-week-old C57BL/6 and \(R a g1^{-/-}\) male and female mice for experiments. We used 8- to 10-week-old \(R a g2^{-/-}/I I2r g^{-/-}\) male and female mice as the recipients. Mice were housed in the SPF-grade animal facility of the Guangzhou Institutes of Biomedicine and Health, Chinese Academy of Sciences (GIBH, CAS, China). All animal experiments were approved by the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee of Guangzhou Institutes of Biomedicine and Health (IACUC-GIBH).
## Cell lines
Cell linesMouse embryonic fibroblasts (MEFs) were derived from 13.5 d.p.c.C57BL/6 mouse embryos. MEFs were maintained in DMEM/high glucose (Gibco), \(10\%\) FBS (Natocor) supplemented with \(1\%\) nonessential amino acids (NEAA, Gibco). The Runx1-p2a-Hoxa9-ESCs constructed (Guo et al., 2020) before were maintained on feeder layers in ES medium containing DMEM/high glucose, \(15\%\) FBS (Gibco), \(1\%\) NEAA (Gibco), \(1\%\) GlutaMAX (Gibco), \(1\%\) Sodium Pyruvate (Gibco), \(0.1\,\mathsf{\ m M}\) \(\upbeta\) -mercaptoethanol (Sigma), \(1~\upmu\mathsf{M}\) PD0325901 (Selleck), \(3~\upmu\mathsf{M}\) Chir99021 (Selleck), and \(1000\ \cup/\mathsf{m L}\) LIF. OP9-DL1 cells \((\mathsf{G F P}^{+})\) were maintained in \(\pmb{\alpha}\) -MEM (Gibco) supplemented with \(20\%\) FBS (Ausbian). The AFT024 cell lines (ATCC) were maintained in DMEM/high glucose, \(10\%\) FBS (Natocor) supplemented with \(0.1\,\mathsf{m M}\,\upbeta\) -mercaptoethanol and \(1\%\) Sodium Pyruvate. MEFs, Runx1-p2a-Hoxa9-ESCs, and OP9-DL1 cells were maintained at \(37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(5\%\) \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) . AFT024 cell lines were maintained at \(33^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(5\%\) \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) .
## METHOD DETAILS
## Hematopoietic differentiation
METHOD DETAILSHematopoietic differentiationRunx1-p2a-Hoxa9-ESCs were trypsinized by \(0.05\%\) Trypsin-EDTA (Gibco) and resuspended in the basic differentiation medium (BDM: IMDM, \(15\%\) FBS (Gibco), \(200\ \upmu\mathfrak{g}/\mathfrak{m L}\) iron-saturated transferrin (Sigma), \(0.1\ \mathsf{m M}\ \upbeta\) -mercaptoethanol (Sigma), \(1\%\) GlutaMAX (Gibco), and \(50\ \upmu\mathfrak{g}/\mathfrak{m L}\) ascorbic acid (Sigma)). To remove the feeder layers, the ESCs were plated into the \(0.1\%\) gelatin-coated (Merck Millipore) well, and the floating cells were collected after 30–40 min. For embryoid body (EB) generation, the ESCs were resuspended at \(100,000\,\mathsf{c e l s}/\mathsf{m L}\) in the BDM supplemented with \(5\,\mathsf{n g}/\mathsf{m L}\) BMP4 (Peprotech) and plated at \(20\,\upmu\mathrm{L}/\) drop for inverted culture in \(15\,\mathsf{c m}\) dishes. At day 2.5, EBs were replanted into gelatinized plates in BDM supplemented with \(5\,\mathsf{n g}/\mathsf{m L}\) BMP4 and \(5\,\mathsf{n g}/\mathsf{m L}\) VEGF (Peprotech). At day 6, the medium was changed to BDM supplemented with \(2\%\) conditioned medium derived from the supernatants of AFT024-mIL3, AFT024-mIL6, AFT024-hFlt3L, and AFT024-mSCF cell culture. Doxycycline \((1\,\upmu\mathfrak{g}/\mathfrak{m L}\) , Sigma) was added at day 6. The medium was replaced every other day. The plates were seeded with OP9-DL1 cells (20,000 cells/well, 12-well plate) \(12\,\mathfrak{h}\) prior to the hematopoietic maturation step in educated medium (EM: \(\pmb{\alpha}\) -MEM, \(15\%\) DFBS (Hyclone), \(200\,\upmu\mathfrak{g}/\mathfrak{m L}\) iron-saturated transferrin, \(0.1\ \mathsf{m M}\ \upbeta\) -mercaptoethanol (Sigma), \(1\%\) GlutaMAX, \(50\ \upmu\mathfrak{g}/\mathfrak{m L}\) ascorbic acid, \(2\%\) conditioned medium derived from supernatants of AFT024-mIL3, AFT024-hFlt3L, and AFT024-mSCF cell culture and \(1\ \upmu\mathfrak{g}/\mathfrak{m L}\) doxycycline). Then, 2,000–3,000 sorted iHECs were seeded into each well for hematopoietic maturation. The EM medium was half-replaced every two days.
## Transplantation of iHPCs
Transplantation of iHPCsEight-ten-week-old \(R a g2^{-/-}/I I2r g^{-/-}\) mice were sublethally irradiated (2.25 Gy) by an X-ray irradiator (RS2000, Rad Source Inc.). Three million iR9-ESCs-derived iHPCs were injected into each irradiated \(R a g2^{-/-}/I I2r g^{-/-}\) mouse via retro-orbital veins. The mice were fed with water containing co-trimoxazole (Tianjin Lisheng Pharmaceutical co., LTD) for two weeks to prevent infection and containing doxycycline \((1\mathrm{~mg/mL})\) to induce the ILC generation.
## Isolation of leukocytes
The intestine was harvested and cleaned of fat residue. To obtain intestinal cells, the intestine was cut longitudinally and scraped in phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) supplemented with \(2\%\) fetal bovine serum (2% FBS) to clean the mucus and feces. The clean tissue was fragmented into pieces in DMEM (Gibco) medium containing Type IV collagenase (1 mg/mL, BasalMedia) and incubated in a shaker at \(37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 15 min in order to disaggregate the tissue. The intestinal tissue fragments were filtered by \(70\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) cell strainer and enriched by centrifugation (5 min, \(500\times\mathfrak{g}\), \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). Whole lung was directly fragmented into pieces in DMEM medium containing Type IV collagenase (1 mg/mL, BasalMedia) and incubated in a shaker at \(37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 15 min, then was filtered by \(70\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) cell strainer and enriched by centrifugation (5 min, \(500\times\mathfrak{g}\), \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). The tissues of liver, spleen, and thymus were mechanically dissociated into single-cell suspension by grinding. Cells were then filtered through a \(70\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) cell strainer and re-suspended into PBS containing \(2\%\) FBS after centrifugation (5 min, \(500\times\mathfrak{g}\), \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)).
For obtaining bone marrow cells, tibia and femur were crushed in PBS containing \(2\%\) FBS and penicillin-streptomycin (100 U/mL). The single-cell suspension was filtered through a \(70\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) cell strainer and washed once with \(5\;\mathrm{mL}\) of the same medium. That was followed by RBC lysis by ammonium chloride.
## Flow cytometry and cell sorting
The single cells were blocked by Fc (CD16/32) (93, eBioscience) antibody, and then stained with related antibodies. The following antibodies were used: CD31 (390, eBioscience), CD41 (eBioMWReg30, eBioscience), CD45 (30-F11, eBioscience), CD201 (eBio1560, eBioscience), C-Kit (2B8, eBioscience), Sca1 (D7, eBioscience), CD45.2 (104, eBioscience), CD3 (145-2C11, eBioscience), CD4 (Gk1.5, Biolegend), CD8 (53–6.7, Biolegend), CD5 (53–7.3, Biolegend), CD27 (LG.3A10, Biolegend), CD19 (6D5, Biolegend), B220 (RA3-6B2, eBioscience), CD11b (M170, eBioscience), CD11c (117,310, Biolegend), NK1.1 (PK136, eBioscience), Ter119 (TER-119, eBioscience), Gr1 (RB6-8C5, eBioscience), TCRβ (H57-597, eBioscience), CD127 (A7R34, eBioscience), RORγt (Q31-378, BD Biosciences), KLRG1 (2F1, Biolegend), NKp46 (29A1.4, eBioscience), CD49a (Ha31/8, BD Biosciences), CD49b (DX5, eBioscience), Gata3 (TWAJ, eBioscience), CD90.2 (53–2.1, eBioscience), ST2 (RMST2-2, eBioscience), IL-17A (TC11-18H10.1, Biolegend), IL-22 (1H8PWSR, eBioscience), IFN-γ (XMG1.2, Biolegend), CD135 (A2F10, eBioscience), CD25 (PC61.5, eBioscience), IL-13 (eBio13A, eBioscience), IL-5 (TRFK5, eBioscience), \(\alpha4\beta\) (DATK32, BD Biosciences), Streptavidin APC-Cy7 (Biolegend). The cells were resuspended in the DAPI solution or PI solution (Biolegend) and were analyzed with Fortessa cytometer (BD Biosciences). For transcription factor staining, after cell surface staining, cells were incubated with Fixation Buffer (Fixo2/Transcription Factor Staining Buffer Set, eBioscience) in the dark for 40 min at room temperature and washed with \(2\;\mathrm{mL}\) of \(1\times\) Permeabilization Wash Buffer, followed by staining for 40 min in the dark at room temperature with fluorochrome-conjugated antibodies against transcription factors (RORγt, Gata3). For intracellular cytokine (IFN-γ, IL-5, IL-13, IL-22 and IL-17A) staining, which was similar to the transcription factor staining, after cell surface staining, cells were incubated with fixation buffer (Fixation & Permeabilization buffer set, Invitrogen) for 30 min at room temperature and washed with \(2\;\mathrm{mL}\) of \(1\times\) Permeabilization Wash Buffer. Then cells were stained with antibodies against cytokines in the dark for 30 min at room temperature. Cells were analyzed on the LSRFortessa cell analyser (BD) or sorted on the Arial II cytometer (BD Biosciences) or Arial III cytometer (BD Biosciences). Flow cytometry data were analyzed with FlowJo (Three Star, Ashland OR).
## In vitro ILC culture assay
For in vitro culture, R9-ILCs \((2\times10^{4}/\mathrm{mL})\) and WT-ILCs \((2\times10^{4}/\mathrm{mL})\) were sorted from the intestine or spleen of \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice at 3 weeks after transplantation and wild type mice (C57BL/6, CD45.2) respectively. Cells sorted from intestine were cultured in RPMI-1640 medium containing \(15\%\) FBS, IL-7 \((20\mathrm{~ng/mL})\), and IL-2 \((10\mathrm{~ng/mL})\), IL-12 \((50\mathrm{~ng/mL})\), IL-15 \((50\mathrm{~ng/mL})\), and IL-23 \((50\mathrm{~ng/mL})\) were also added into the culture medium to stimulate R9-iILC1/3s and WT-ILC1/3s. Cells sorted from spleen were cultured in RPMI-1640 medium containing \(15\%\) FBS, IL-7 \((20\mathrm{~ng/mL})\), and IL-2 \((10\mathrm{~ng/mL})\), IL-25 \((50\mathrm{~ng/mL})\) and IL-33 \((50\mathrm{~ng/mL})\) were added into the culture medium to stimulate R9-iILC2s and WT-ILC2s. All cells were collected after \(40\;\mathrm{h}\) of culture and analyzed by flow cytometry.
## Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)
Mouse IL-22 (R&D Systems) in culture supernatants of WT-ILC3s and R9-iILC3s were quantified by ELISA according to the manufacturer's instructions. Supernatants were collected by centrifugation (5 min, \(500\times\mathfrak{g}\), \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)).
## Cytokine injections
The \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice were injected intraperitoneally on 4 consecutive days with 500 ng IL-12 and IL-15 at 4 weeks after transplanted three million R9-iHPCs. PBS was injected to the recipients as the negative control. Mice were sacrificed \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) after the last injection for flow cytometry.
## Genotype sequencing
Individual iILC was sorted into PBS using ArialI sorter (BD Biosciences). The single cell genome was amplified by Discover-sc single cell kit according to manufacturer's protocols (N601-02, Vazyme Biotech). After 35 cycles of amplification (30 s at \(95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), 1 min at \(55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), 1 min at \(72^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)), PCR products were purified and sequenced. PCR for detecting Runx1-p2a-Hoxa9 fragment was performed as described using the following primers: F-P (upstream): CAACTTCCTCTGCTCCGTGC; R-P(downstream): CGTCGTCCTTTGCTCGGTCCT. The PCR products of iILCs were sequenced and confirmed.
## RNA-seq and data analysis
The cDNA of single R9-iILCs, WT-ILCs, R9-iCHILRs, and WT-CHILRs were generated and amplified using Discover-sc WTA Kit V2 (Vazyme). The quality of amplified cDNA was assessed by qPCR analysis of housekeeping genes (Gapd)n. Samples that passed quality control were used for sequencing library preparation using TruePrep DNA Library Prep Kit V2 (Vazyme). All libraries were sequenced by illumina sequencer NextSeq 500. The raw data (fastq files) were generated using bcl2fastq software (version 2.16.0.10) and were uploaded to the Gene Expression Omnibus public database (GEO: GSE186777 and GEO: GSE210861). The clean reads were aligned to the mm10 mouse transcriptome (UCSC) by HISAT2 (version 2.2.0) (Kim et al., 2019). Gene expression levels were transcripts per million (TPM) calculated by StringTie software (v 2.1.4) (Shumate et al., 2022). Heat maps were plotted using pheatmap (version 1.0.12). The violin plots exhibited the expression distribution of marker genes were implemented by VInplot of Seurat (Stuart et al., 2019).
30,000 sorted early bone marrow iR9-ESCs-derived progenitors \((\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}C D2^{-}C D3^{-}C D4^{-}C D8^{-}C D19^{-}T e r119^{-}G r1^{-}}\) CD11bNK1.1) from iILC regenerated \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice \((\mathsf{n}\,=\,9)\) at day 7.5 after transplantation were used for scRNA-seq. Droplet-based scRNA-seq dataset was produced using a chromium system ( \(10~\times\) Genomics, PN120263) following manufacture's instruction.
30,000 sorted early bone marrow iR9-ESCs-derived progenitors \((\mathsf{C D45.2^{+}G F P^{+}C D2^{-}C D3^{-}C D4^{-}C D8^{-}C D19^{-}T e r119^{-}G r1^{-}}\) CD11bNK1.1) from iILC regenerated \(R a g2^{-/-}I I2r g^{-/-}\) recipient mice \((\mathsf{n}\,=\,9)\) at day 7.5 after transplantation were used for scRNA-seq. Droplet-based scRNA-seq dataset was produced using a chromium system ( \(10~\times\) Genomics, PN120263) following manufacture's instruction.Droplet-based scRNA-seq dataset was uploaded to the Gene Expression Omnibus public database (GEO: GSE186776). The data were firstly aligned to the Mus musculus genome (mm10) and quantified using the Cell Ranger software package (version 3.0.0, \(10~\times\) Genomics), and then subjected to Seurat (version 3.2.3) for further analysis. To pass quality control, cells should have at least 200 and no more than 7,500 genes detected, and less than \(5\%\) of mapped reads mapping to mitochondrial genes. Finally, 7,391 cells passed the quality control. Then, PCA was performed by RunPCA using 2,000 highly variable genes and the top 30 PCs were used for clustering and Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection (UMAP) analysis. The most variable PCs were determined by the Jackstraw method. Clusters were detected using FindClusters with setting parameters dims \(=1{:}30\), resolution \(=0.1\). We next extracted the populations (cluster 0, 2, and 3 in Figure 5A) with highly enriched expression of Id2 (more than \(50\%\) of cells in each population with UMIs \(>1\)) and performed analysis. PCA was performed by RunPCA using 2,000 highly variable genes and the top 20 PCs were used for clustering and UMAP analysis. Clusters were detected using ''Find-Neighbors'' and ''FindClusters'' functions with setting resolution \(=0.2\) and annotated via differential expressions of ILC population-related marker genes. UMAP analysis was performed using the ''RunUMAP'' function in Seurat, with 20 PCs and other default parameters. Up-regulated genes in each cluster were identified using Wilcoxon rank-sum test with parameter 'min.pct \(=0.25\), logfc.threshold \(=1\). The heatmap showing the expression of top 20 differential up-expression genes across cell populations was plotted by pheatmap package (version 1.0.12). The violin plots exhibited the expression distribution of iILC-progenitor related genes and chemokine receptor genes were implemented by VInplot of Seurat. The dot plot to present the average-expression of marker genes was drawed by ggplot2 package (v 3.3.3). Normalization of UMI value was applied using 'LogNormalize' method in Seurat.
Pseudotime analysis was performed using the monocle3 (v3) (Qiu et al., 2017). The UMAP analysis results and 'LogNormalize' normalized expression profile from previous analysis were used as input for construction the pseudotime trajectories of the iILC-related progenitors (cluster C1-C4 or cluster C1-C5). These cells were ordered by cluster_cells and learn_graph functions with resolution \(=0.0001\). A selected cell belonged to the C1 cluster was assigned as the root node using the ''order_cells helper'' function. Following construction of the pseudotime trajectory, the ''graph_test'' function was used to identify genes whose expression varied with pseudotime (q value \(<10^{-2}\), pseudotime variable genes). These genes were then segregated into several expression profile modules by ''find_gene_modules'' function (resolution \(=10^{-2}\), random_seed \(=12\)). Kinetics plots showed the expression profile across the single-cell trajectory using ggplot2 package with 'geom_smooth (se \(=\mathsf{F}\), method \(=\) ''loess\)')'. The gene expression value was normalized by ''Lognormalize'' method in Seurat package. The pseudo-time value assigned by pseudotime trajectory analysis was extracted via 'cds@principal_graph_aux@listData\\(UMAP\\)pseudotime'. Finally, gene ontology (GO)-enrichment analysis for the co-expressed genes in the modules were performed by clusterProfiler package (version 3.14.3) (Yu et al., 2012) with BH adjusted p value cutoff \(=0.05\).
was used for prep step of integration. Then, "FindIntegrationAnchors" function was used for finding a set of anchors between two scRNA-seq datasets. Finally, "IntegrateData" function and the standard workflow for clustering were performed using the top 10 PCs with setting resolution \(=0.2\).
## QUANTIFICATION AND STATISTICAL ANALYSIS
